<header><p class="st-kicker">county · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Évreux</h1><p class="st-meta">989 CE – 1404 CE (approx.) · capital Évreux</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Norman county that became the appanage of a royal branch whose members, as kings of Navarre, schemed against the Valois.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 989 Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-normandy">Normandy</a> created the county of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evreux">Évreux</a> for his son Robert, archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouen">Rouen</a>. Robert's descendants held it as major Norman barons, and it later passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-montfort">Montfort</a> family, frequently caught up in the wars between Normandy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evreux">Évreux</a> was a rich farming district on the road from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouen">Rouen</a>, with a cathedral city as its centre. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-france">Philip II</a> obtained it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">John of England</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-le-goulet">Treaty of Le Goulet</a> in 1200. In 1307 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-france">Philip IV</a> gave it as an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> to his half-brother Louis, whose son married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joan-ii-of-navarre">Joan II of Navarre</a>, founding the Évreux kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-navarre">Charles the Bad</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evreux">Évreux</a> as a base for his intrigues against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-ii-of-france">John II</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>, who seized most of his Norman lands. In 1404 his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iii-of-navarre">Charles III of Navarre</a> exchanged Évreux with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-vi-of-france">Charles VI</a> for the duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-nemours">Nemours</a>, and it returned to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-evreux.jpg" alt="County of Évreux" width="320" height="230"><figcaption>Image: PurpleHz · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carte_Normandie_Historique.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>989 CE – 1404 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Évreux</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Robert of Rouen, Louis of Évreux, Charles II of Navarre</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Évreux</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1196&amp;focus=county-of-evreux" aria-label="County of Évreux on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4707 467 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Évreux lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5027 723L5025 711M5027 723L4960 913M5027 723L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5025 711h0M4960 913h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5027 723h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5027 723h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Évreux. <a href="/a